.\" -*-nroff-*-
.TH POWWOW 6 2026-11-19 "powwow @PACKAGE_VERSION@"
.SH NAME
powwow \- telnet protocol client for MUD playing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powwow
.RI "[" options "] [" definition-file "] [" host " " port "]"
.br
.B powwow
.RB [ \-\-config [= \fIfile\fR]]
.BI \-\-get " name"
.RI [ definition-file ]
.br
.B powwow
.RB [ \-\-config [= \fIfile\fR]]
.B \-\-list\-settings
.RI [ definition-file ]
.br
.B powwow
.RB [ \-\-config [= \fIfile\fR]]
.BI \-\-set " name" = value
.RI [ definition-file ]
.br
.B powwow
.BI \-\-completion " shell"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powwow
is a client program that implements the telnet protocol, adding some
bells and whistles to the standard
.BR telnet (1)
communication utility, mainly intended for playing multi-user games
such as
.IR MUDs " (" "Multi-User Dungeons" ")."
.PP
.B powwow
is either invoked with
.I host
and
.IR port ,
and then does not save any definitions, or with a
.IR definition-file ,
which is supposed to contain various definitions as well as the name
of the host and the port number.  If the definition-file does not
exist, the user is prompted for the host name and port number which
are saved in the new file.  The definition file can be edited with any
text editor, and is updated whenever a definition (action, alias,
etc.) is changed.  If the environment variable
.B POWWOWDIR
is defined,
.B powwow
will look there first for definition files (and create new ones
there).
.PP
Before the definition file,
.B powwow
reads the user-wide settings file, which has the settings for all MUDs;
see
.B USER-WIDE SETTINGS FILE
below.
.PP
Lines that begin with \[oq]#\[cq] are assumed to be special commands
to be processed locally.  Help is available with the
.I #help
command; the complete manual is the file
.IR powwow.md ,
installed in the same directory as the help text; the launcher also
shows it.  Notably,
semicolons (\[oq];\[cq]) separate commands on a single line.  To send
a literal semicolon, prefix it with a backslash: \[oq]\e;\[cq].
.PP
.B powwow
also supports the MUME client protocol for editing and viewing the
texts of MUME locally.
The name of the editor is taken from the environment variable
.B POWWOWEDITOR
or
.BR EDITOR ;
.BR vi (1)
is used by default.  For text viewing,
.B POWWOWPAGER
is searched, then
.B PAGER
and
.BR more (1)
is used by default;
.B #help
searches
.B POWWOWHELPPAGER
first.  The command
.B #!
runs its command with
.BR sh (1),
or gives it to the shell in
.BR POWWOWSHELL ,
such as
.RB \(lq "zsh -c" \(rq,
as its last argument, as do the commands after
.B !
of
.B #var
and others.  The options
.BR "#option editor=" ,
.BR pager= ,
.B help-pager=
and
.B shell=
come before these variables.  A pager of
.RB \(lq \- \(rq
(a hyphen) is none:
.B #help
prints its text as other messages, and the texts that the MUD sends to
view are printed as its other lines.
.PP
If the first character of the editor or the pager is \[oq]&\[cq]
(which is then skipped), it runs in a window of its own, and
.B powwow
goes on running without waiting for it.  The editor must not end before
the text is saved and closed, or
.B powwow
ends the editing session at once: give it the option that makes it
wait, such as
.B \-\-wait
for Visual Studio Code.  Such an editor reads its standard input from
.IR /dev/null ,
and what it writes to its standard output and standard error is
shown as lines beginning with \[oq]#editor:\[cq].
.PP
When launched, the editor program will have its
.B TITLE
environment variable set to the document title sent from the MUD.
.PP
.B powwow
handles text internally as Unicode (UTF-8), and converts it to and
from the character set of the terminal\[cq]s locale.  The connection to the
server can use UTF-8 (the default), ISO-8859-1 or US-ASCII, as negotiated
using the telnet CHARSET option (RFC 2066) or selected with
.BR \-\-latin1 .
Invalid input is shown as U+FFFD; characters that cannot be represented
are shown or sent as \[oq]?\[cq].
Captured, recorded and edited files use the connection\[cq]s character set;
definition files are UTF-8, and older ISO-8859-1 ones are converted when
loaded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
display help text and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\fR[=\fItls-options\fR]
use encrypted TLS connections (the default); this overrides the TLS
setting of the definition file\[cq]s host; additional
.IR tls-options ,
separated by colons (\[oq]:\[cq]), are:
.RS
.TP
.B no-verify
do not validate the CA certificate
.TP
.BI host= HOST
assume the host name to be
.I HOST
when validating the CA certificate
.RE
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-tls
use unencrypted connections; this overrides the TLS setting of the
definition file\[cq]s host
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-latin1
start the connection in ISO-8859-1 and prefer ISO-8859-1 when negotiating
the character set with the server
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utf8
start the connection in UTF-8 and prefer UTF-8 when negotiating the
character set with the server (the default); this overrides the character
set of the definition file\[cq]s host
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
display the
.B powwow
version and exit; given twice, also display the credits and the options
.B powwow
was compiled with
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-windows
on Cygwin, take file names and commands as on Windows, as the
.B powwow
of the installer for Windows does: a file name after
.B <
and a command after
.B !
keep their backslashes, and the
.BI % NAME %
of environment variables in file names are replaced by their values,
and messages and
.B #help
write environment variables so;
the editor and the pager run as
.B #!
runs its commands, with the shell of
.BR "#option shell" ,
followed by the Windows path of the file, and are Notepad by default,
or nano in an ssh session, with the shell powwow-cmd, which runs
the commands with cmd, where they are found; a
.B \e\e
in double quotes in those commands, and in those of
.BR #! ,
is one backslash, as it is for sh; Tab completes file names
in any case, with a backslash after folders; a file name that
Windows does not allow, with
.B :
but after a drive letter, one of
.BR "\(dq * < > ? |" ,
a name ending in a dot or a space, or that of a device such as
.BR NUL ,
is an error; and
.B #spawn
is not available
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR[=\fIfile\fR]
save the user-wide settings in their own file, or in
.I file
if given, which is also read instead of the user-wide settings file
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
do not read the user-wide settings file
.TP
.BI \-\-get " name"
print the setting
.I name
on standard output and exit, after reading the user-wide settings file
and the definition file, if given: a command option, such as
.BR editor ,
an on/off option, such as
.BR save ,
as
.B on
or
.BR off ,
.BR host ,
as
.B #host
takes it, the user-wide attributes of
.B #hilite input
and
.BR "#hilite messages" ,
.B hilite\-input
and
.BR hilite\-messages ,
as
.B #hilite
takes them, or nothing for none, which
.B \-\-set
sets back to those of powwow with
.BR default ,
and the MUD's own,
.B mud\-hilite\-input
and
.BR mud\-hilite\-messages ,
or
.B default
when it has none, or the path of the user-wide settings
file,
.BR config\-file ,
or of the manual,
.BR manual ,
or
.BR in\-use ,
the process ID of a
.B powwow
that has the definition file open, when
.B \-\-set
fails, or nothing
.TP
.B \-\-list\-settings
print all the settings of
.BR \-\-get ,
one per line as
.IB name = value\fR,
and exit
.TP
.BI \-\-set " name" = value
set the setting
.IR name ,
as for
.BR \-\-get ,
but not the paths,
save the settings and exit, as with
.BR \-\-config :
a user-wide setting, such as
.BR editor ,
goes in the user-wide settings file, unless
.B \-\-no\-config
is given, and the others need a definition file; one that does not
exist is created without asking for the host
.TP
.BI \-\-completion " shell"
print the completion of the arguments of
.B powwow
in
.IR shell ,
.B bash
or
.BR zsh ,
and exit; it completes the options, those of
.BR \-\-tls= ,
the file of
.BR \-\-config= ,
the settings of
.B \-\-get
and
.B \-\-set
and their values, and the definition files, those that end with
.B .powwow
unless none does, also those in
.BR POWWOWDIR .
.B make install
installs the one of bash where the package bash-completion loads it;
else load it in
.I ~/.bashrc
with
.BR "eval \(dq$(powwow \-\-completion bash)\(dq" ,
and that of zsh likewise in
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
after
.B compinit
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
Your terminal type.
.TP
.B POWWOWDIR
Where
.B powwow
will store and search for its files by default.
.TP
.BR POWWOWEDITOR " or " EDITOR
Name of editor to use.
.TP
.BR POWWOWPAGER " or " PAGER
Name of pager to use;
.B \-
for none.
.TP
.B POWWOWHELPPAGER
Name of pager to use for
.BR #help ,
instead of the one above.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the editor and the pager get the temporary file of the text;
.I /tmp
if not set.
.SH USER-WIDE SETTINGS FILE
The user-wide settings file has the settings for all MUDs: the command
options, such as
.BR "#option editor=" ,
the secrets of
.BR #secret ,
.BR "#option save" ,
and the attributes of
.BR #hilite .
.B powwow
reads it before the definition file, which can override them.  When
.B powwow
saves, these settings go in the definition file, unless it was started
with
.BR \-\-config ,
as the launcher starts it: then they go in the user-wide settings file,
at once when one changes, and
.B powwow
reads it again when another program, such as the launcher, writes it.
.B \-\-config=\fIfile\fR
reads and saves
.I file
instead, and
.B \-\-no\-config
does not read it.
.PP
It is in the folder where each system keeps the settings of programs:
.\" each path and the words with it on lines of their own, as justified
.\" lines with them have wide gaps, and the paths not hyphenated
.TP
Linux and FreeBSD
.IR \%$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/powwow/powwow.conf ,
.br
by\ default
.I \%\[ti]/.config/powwow/powwow.conf
.TP
macOS
.RI \(dq "\%\[ti]/Library/Application \%Support/powwow/powwow.conf" \(dq,
.br
or
.I \%\[ti]/.config/powwow/powwow.conf
.br
if
.I \%\[ti]/.config/powwow
exists
.TP
Windows
.I \%%APPDATA%\epowwow\epowwow.conf
.SH FILES
.TP \w'$POWWOWDIR/*\ \ \ 'u
powwow
The binary.
.TP
$POWWOWDIR/*
Definition files for aliases, etc.
.TP
powwow.conf
The user-wide settings file; see
.B USER-WIDE SETTINGS FILE
above.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages from
.B powwow
are prefixed with a \[oq]#\[cq] to separate them from the output
of the remote host.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs as issues and send patches as pull requests at
.UR https://github.com/MUME/powwow
GitHub
.UE .
.SH AUTHORS
.B powwow
is maintained at
.UR https://github.com/MUME/powwow
GitHub
.UE .
.PP
.MT max@Linuz.sns.it
Massimiliano Ghilardi
.ME
and Gustav H\[oa]llberg are the people that added many new features
to an existing client,
.B Cancan
and transformed it into
.BR powwow .
.MT f91-men@nada.kth.se
Mattias Engdeg\[oa]rd
.ME
was the original author of
.B Cancan
but many other people have
contributed at least as much, including
.MT finnag@pvv.unit.no
Finn Arne Gangstad
.ME ,
.MT dgay@di.epfl.ch
David Gay
.ME ,
.MT gdezern@satelnet.org
Gary Dezern
.ME
and
.MT vivriel@scs.com.sg
Lai-Chang Woo
.ME .
.PP
Steve Slaven contributed as the maintainer from 2005.
.PP
.B powwow
was originally inspired by Peter Unold\[cq]s
.B Tintin
client, but now only bears a very remote resemblance to the original.
It aims to be a simple but very interactive client, and contains
features that are interesting for \[lq]immortal\[rq] players, in
particular on MUME, though any mud player should benefit from
.B powwow
as well.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR telnet (1),
.BR more (1),
.BR vi (1)
